        "thought_text": "Thought-3: Recursive Backtracking with Memoization\n\nTo correctly solve this problem, we need to use a recursive backtracking approach with memoization. The idea is to build permutations incrementally while ensuring that each partial permutation has runs of length at most \\( k \\). We will use a helper function that takes the current state of the permutation and recursively tries adding each possible next element. If adding an element violates the run length constraint, we backtrack. To optimize, we will use memoization to store results of subproblems and avoid redundant calculations. This approach ensures that we efficiently count all valid permutations without generating them explicitly. Additionally, we need to initialize the `last` parameter to a value that represents no previous element, such as `-1`, to handle the base case correctly.",
